# Zero-touch startup and automatic bootstrap plan

This checklist is the implementation source of truth for making SoulSearch
work immediately after installation without requiring the player to run
`soulsearch`, `enable soulsearch`, or any other initialization command first.

The normal first-use path must be:

1. install the packaged SoulSearch mod;
2. start or restart DFHack and load a fortress; and
3. press the default `Ctrl-F` binding. Once the separately planned Stats
   popover is present, its unit-card overlay button is also discovered without
   manual initialization.

The public command split remains unchanged:

- `gui/soulsearch` opens the full SoulSearch window;
- `soulsearch` performs idempotent runtime setup/validation, retries an
  incomplete first-run setup, and supports `reload`; and
- DFHack's automatic enableable-module scan performs minimal safe bootstrap
  before either command is manually invoked.

The unit-card Stats overlay itself remains owned by
`docs/stats-popover.todo`. This plan owns automatic bootstrap, default-keybind
first-run behavior, native `enable`/`disable` semantics, and their reload and
packaging contracts. Complete this plan before treating the Stats-popover
package and clean-install acceptance gates as release-ready; Stats component
and popover work that does not depend on startup may proceed independently.

Record completed phase results in the `Execution evidence` table at the end of
this document. Every completed phase gate must have a dated entry identifying
the commands, tests, package paths, or live DFHack observations that justify
marking it complete.

## Target behavior

- [ ] A clean packaged installation automatically makes the default
      `Ctrl-F@dwarfmode/Default -> gui/soulsearch` binding available without a
      manual SoulSearch command.
- [ ] Automatic bootstrap never opens a SoulSearch screen, collects units,
      runs a search, constructs the UI, or prints routine startup output.
- [ ] `gui/soulsearch` remains a defensive entry point: it performs any
      required idempotent runtime initialization and then opens the full UI even
      when automatic bootstrap was missed, disabled, or installed mid-session.
- [ ] `soulsearch` remains a safe manual runtime setup/validation command that
      retries incomplete first-run setup without resetting an established
      keybinding opt-out. It does not open UI; `soulsearch reload` remains
      explicit development reconstruction and also does not reopen UI.
- [ ] Existing valid `gui/soulsearch` bindings are treated as user-owned and
      are never replaced, duplicated, or removed by bootstrap.
- [ ] A player who intentionally removes the seeded default through
      `gui/keybinds` does not have it silently recreated on later startups.
- [ ] A missing or incomplete DFHack hotkey API cannot abort DFHack startup;
      setup remains retryable through a later automatic or explicit path.
- [ ] `enable soulsearch` idempotently enables or refreshes SoulSearch's
      automatic bootstrap service for the current DFHack session.
- [ ] `disable soulsearch` stops future automatic bootstrap work for the
      current session without closing screens, deleting user bindings, changing
      overlay configuration, or preventing explicit `soulsearch` and
      `gui/soulsearch` commands.
- [ ] `isEnabled()` reports whether the automatic bootstrap service is active,
      not whether a SoulSearch window or overlay widget is visible.
- [ ] The unit-card Stats widget remains independently controlled by the global
      overlay framework and its own saved widget state. SoulSearch bootstrap
      must not call `enable overlay`, overwrite widget placement, or repurpose
      the full-window `Ctrl-F` binding for the popover.
- [ ] Normal automatic startup is supported from the packaged
      `scripts_modinstalled` tree. A development-only script path, manual
      `require()`, manual overlay rescan, or user init-file entry is not a
      release dependency.

## Architectural constraints

- [ ] Keep the public enableable entry point at
      `src/scripts_modinstalled/soulsearch.lua`. Do not expose a second
      `enable internal/...` command solely for bootstrap.
- [ ] Declare the public script with the exact annotation syntax proven against
      the target DFHack parser. The expected repository-consistent form is
      `--@module=true` and `--@enable=true`; Phase 0 must verify it before
      package checks depend on a literal spelling.
- [ ] Keep bootstrap narrower than `initialize()`:
  - [ ] load only the modules required for startup setup;
  - [ ] do not validate or eagerly load the complete `module_registry.lua`
        graph;
  - [ ] do not load `ui.lua` merely to seed a keybinding; and
  - [ ] retain full registry validation for explicit initialization, GUI open,
        and reload paths.
- [ ] Prefer no world-state callback for the first implementation. The current
      `lifecycle.prepare_for_world()` only invalidates caches and remains safe
      to call lazily when a GUI or popover actually needs runtime data.
- [ ] Add an `onStateChange` hook only if Phase 0 proves a concrete startup
      requirement that cannot be handled by module-load bootstrap and defensive
      entry-point initialization. If added, it must:
  - [ ] use a stable SoulSearch-owned key;
  - [ ] be attached at top level before the module-mode return so it can receive
        `SC_DFHACK_INITIALIZED` during the cold-start scan;
  - [ ] register and unregister idempotently;
  - [ ] resolve reloadable modules dynamically instead of retaining stale
        `reqscript()` tables; and
  - [ ] do no unit collection or UI construction from the callback.
- [ ] Branch invocation modes explicitly so enable/disable flags cannot fall
      through to ordinary command parsing:
  - [ ] `dfhack_flags.enable` handles `enable_state`;
  - [ ] `dfhack_flags.module` defaults bootstrap to enabled only when the
        script environment has no prior explicit state, performs idempotent
        setup when enabled, and returns; and
  - [ ] ordinary invocation dispatches `soulsearch` arguments.
- [ ] Initialize automatic-bootstrap state with an explicit nil check so a
      prior `false` survives script re-execution/reload; do not use an `or true`
      expression that silently re-enables a disabled session.
- [ ] Preserve one exported, idempotent setup seam used by automatic module
      loading, `enable soulsearch`, and manual `soulsearch` initialization.
- [ ] Do not cache reloadable internal module tables in automatic callbacks or
      long-lived bootstrap closures. `soulsearch reload` must not leave a prior
      generation callable.
- [ ] Define automatic enablement as session runtime state. Do not introduce
      save-specific enablement merely to imitate another mod. Any persistence
      added for keybinding first-run ownership must remain a separate global
      preference with a single narrow purpose.
- [ ] Keep first-run keybinding state global rather than fortress-specific: the
      default command and `gui/keybinds` configuration are global DFHack user
      preferences.
- [ ] Do not record the first-run/default-considered marker until the hotkey API
      has been queried successfully and either an existing valid binding was
      observed or the default was added successfully.
- [ ] Treat the following keybinding outcomes as distinct and testable:
  - [ ] `added`: no valid binding or prior decision existed, so the default was
        added and the first-run decision can be recorded;
  - [ ] `existing`: a valid user or previously seeded binding already exists,
        so it is retained and the first-run decision can be recorded;
  - [ ] `opted_out`: the first-run decision exists but no valid binding remains,
        so bootstrap respects the removal;
  - [ ] `unavailable`: the required DFHack API or preference store is not
        usable, so startup fails soft and remains retryable; and
  - [ ] `error`: setup could not complete safely, does not claim success, and
        does not partially overwrite user state.
- [ ] Treat the binding and marker as a recoverable two-step operation, not a
      filesystem transaction: DFHack's JSON writer truncates and writes the
      target file directly. Add the binding before recording the marker; if
      marker writing fails, a retry must recognize the existing binding, record
      the marker, and never add a duplicate.
- [ ] Treat only the supported no-argument `gui/soulsearch` command as the
      default-binding identity. Invalid argument-bearing commands must not mask
      creation of a working default.
- [ ] `disable soulsearch` must not remove any binding in the initial release.
      Binding removal ownership cannot be inferred safely from command text
      alone after the user has edited configuration.

## Phase 0 - Verify DFHack startup and user-configuration behavior

### Evidence recorded on 2026-07-13

- Target runtime: DFHack `53.15-r1 (release)` attached to Dwarf Fortress at
  `G:\Steam\steamapps\common\Dwarf Fortress`.
- The current runtime has the development path
  `+D:/CODE/DFHack/SoulSearch/src/scripts_modinstalled` configured in
  `dfhack-config/script-paths.txt`. No packaged SoulSearch copy is installed,
  so this is not evidence for a packaged cold start.
- The target Lua API documents both `--@module=true`/`--@enable=true` and the
  spaced spelling as valid; use the no-space spelling already used by the
  current SoulSearch script and installed Justice HQ script.
- DFHack documents that enableable modules are loaded before
  `SC_DFHACK_INITIALIZED`. Its `script-manager.lua` scans installed-mod
  `scripts_modinstalled` directories even when no world is loaded; therefore
  an installed SoulSearch mod does not need to be active in a fortress's mod
  list merely to contribute that script path.
- A script added after DFHack initialization misses that scan. The documented
  development/hot-install recovery is `enable` with no arguments or
  `require('script-manager').reload()`; it is not part of clean-install use.
- DFHack's approved global mod-state location is
  `dfhack-config/mods/soulsearch/`, obtained from
  `require('script-manager').getModStatePath('soulsearch')`. Use a versioned
  `default-keybinding.json` there for the global default-considered marker.
  DFHack's bundled `json.encode_file()` opens the target with `'w'` and writes
  directly, so it provides no atomic replacement guarantee. Corrupt or
  interrupted data must remain a conservative, explicitly repairable state.
- Baseline before source changes: `tools/Build.ps1` passed (32 Lua files),
  `tools/Test.ps1` passed (201 tests), and `git diff --check` passed.

