## Phase 3 - Harden command, reload, and missed-startup behavior

- [ ] Add or expand command/startup tests proving:
  - [ ] automatic module loading seeds first-run setup without opening UI;
  - [ ] automatic bootstrap does not call full registry validation;
  - [ ] repeated module loads do not duplicate setup;
  - [ ] enable and disable flags do not reach ordinary argument parsing;
  - [ ] `isEnabled()` follows module, enable, and disable transitions;
  - [ ] module re-entry through `reqscript()`, GUI opening, or reload preserves
        an explicit disabled state;
  - [ ] explicit `soulsearch` remains usable while automatic bootstrap is
        disabled;
  - [ ] `gui/soulsearch` initializes and opens while automatic bootstrap is
        disabled or was missed;
  - [ ] missing hotkey/config APIs do not open UI or throw from automatic
        startup;
  - [ ] an initial `unavailable` result remains retryable: after the API becomes
        available, a later `soulsearch` or `gui/soulsearch` attempt completes
        the first-run decision exactly once without duplicating a binding;
  - [ ] invalid ordinary arguments still report the exact usage contract; and
  - [ ] no callback or reloadable module reference is duplicated or stale.
- [ ] Narrow the existing test named “module API initializes only when
      requested”: module loading may perform minimal bootstrap, but must not
      perform full runtime/world/UI initialization.
- [ ] Make `soulsearch reload` preserve the current automatic enabled/disabled
      state unless explicitly documented otherwise.
- [ ] Ensure reload reconstructs internal modules without clearing or executing
      the active top-level `soulsearch.lua` environment as part of its own
      internal registry sequence.
- [ ] After reload, rerun only idempotent startup setup required to bind the
      fresh generation. Do not recreate an opted-out binding or reopen UI.
- [ ] If a state-change callback exists, prove reload leaves exactly one
      callback and that its next invocation dynamically resolves the current
      module generation.
- [ ] Preserve `gui/soulsearch` as the hot-install and missed-scan fallback.
- [ ] Document the developer recovery commands for scripts added after DFHack's
      initial scan, based on Phase 0 evidence.

### Phase 3 gate

- [ ] Startup, native enable/disable, manual setup, GUI opening, reload, and
      missed-scan recovery have independent tested contracts with no stale
      runtime generation or UI side effect.
- [ ] `tools/Build.ps1`, `tools/Test.ps1`, and `git diff --check` pass.

## Phase 4 - Coordinate optional overlay integration

This phase is an integration follow-up when the separate Stats-popover work is
present. It does not block completion of the zero-touch startup parent item,
which is proven by the clean-install full-window binding and command paths.
Record `Not applicable - Stats popover not implemented` in the evidence table
when closing this plan independently.

- [ ] When the stats-popover work is present, complete its overlay registration
      with a dedicated module-loadable overlay script and
      `default_enabled=true`.
- [ ] Do not add `--@enable=true` to the overlay registration script merely to
      solve automatic keybinding setup; that responsibility belongs to the
      public `soulsearch.lua` bootstrap.
- [ ] Do not add the top-level overlay registration script to SoulSearch's
      internal module registry.
- [ ] Ensure overlay activation resolves and initializes only the runtime needed
      for the popover and remains functional if automatic bootstrap was missed
      or disabled.
- [ ] Keep the global overlay framework's enabled state and the widget's saved
      enabled/position state authoritative.
- [ ] Keep `Ctrl-F` bound only to the full `gui/soulsearch` command. The
      popover's `overlay_trigger()` may be used in a user-created binding but
      receives no competing default binding from SoulSearch.
- [ ] Coordinate `soulsearch reload` with overlay environment clearing and
      rescan exactly as specified by `docs/stats-popover.todo`, while preserving
      the automatic bootstrap state and first-run keybinding decision.

### Phase 4 gate

- [ ] When the Stats-popover feature is present, a clean package exposes both
      access paths without a manual command:
      `Ctrl-F` opens the full UI and the default-enabled unit-card button opens
      the Stats popover when the overlay framework and saved widget state allow
      it.
- [ ] When the Stats-popover feature is present, disabling automatic SoulSearch
      bootstrap does not mutate overlay state,
      and disabling the overlay framework does not mutate bootstrap state.
- [ ] When the Stats-popover feature is present, its Phase 5 automated gate
      passes; otherwise this integration phase is recorded as not applicable.

## Phase 5 - Documentation, package verification, and release gates

- [ ] Update README installation and usage text so the primary path is
      `install -> restart/load DFHack -> Ctrl-F`, with no manual initialization
      step. Add the unit-card button as a second zero-touch path when the
      separate Stats-popover feature is present.
- [ ] Document the exceptional hot-install/missed-scan recovery path separately
      from normal installation.
- [ ] Document command ownership and arguments:
  - [ ] `gui/soulsearch` opens the full UI;
  - [ ] `soulsearch` performs runtime setup/validation and retries only an
        incomplete first-run decision;
  - [ ] `soulsearch reload` reconstructs development runtime state;
  - [ ] `enable soulsearch` activates automatic bootstrap for the session; and
  - [ ] `disable soulsearch` deactivates only automatic bootstrap work.
- [ ] Document keybinding ownership:
  - [ ] the default is seeded once;
  - [ ] existing custom bindings win;
  - [ ] saved removal is respected;
  - [ ] `gui/keybinds` is the supported edit surface; and
  - [ ] disabling bootstrap does not delete bindings.
- [ ] Document the independent overlay layers: global `enable overlay`, saved
      widget enablement/placement, and SoulSearch bootstrap state.
- [ ] Update the Stats-popover README/help work to cross-reference this startup
      contract without duplicating it.
- [ ] Add package verification that the shipped
      `scripts_modinstalled/soulsearch.lua` contains target-verified semantic
      module and enable annotations and that every new preference/bootstrap
      dependency is included. Do not hard-code an unverified alternate
      whitespace spelling.
- [ ] Choose and record the release version before publishing. Update
      `NUMERIC_VERSION` and `DISPLAYED_VERSION` in `src/info.txt` as required.
- [ ] Run the automated closure sequence separately:
  - [ ] `tools/Build.ps1`;
  - [ ] `tools/Test.ps1`;
  - [ ] `tools/Publish.ps1`;
  - [ ] `tools/VerifyPackage.ps1` through the publish workflow; and
  - [ ] `git diff --check`.
- [ ] Record total tests, failures, produced archive path, expanded package
      path, and exact packaged script path in the execution evidence.

### Phase 5 gate

- [ ] Help, README, source, expanded package, and archive describe and contain
      the same zero-touch startup behavior.
- [ ] Automated build, tests, publish, package verification, and whitespace
      checks are green.

## Phase 6 - Clean-install live acceptance

- [ ] Use a clean test profile or isolate and later restore the user's real
      keybinding, SoulSearch preference, and overlay configuration.
- [ ] Disable the repository development script path and remove or isolate
      competing installed SoulSearch versions so the packaged payload is the
      only resolvable copy.
- [ ] Install the packaged mod and cold-start DFHack without invoking
      `soulsearch`, `gui/soulsearch`, `enable soulsearch`, `enable` with no
      arguments, `script-manager.reload()`, or an overlay rescan.
- [ ] Before any manual SoulSearch command:
  - [ ] confirm `enable` lists SoulSearch with the automatic bootstrap active;
  - [ ] confirm exactly one valid `gui/soulsearch` binding exists;
  - [ ] press `Ctrl-F` in fortress mode and confirm the full window opens; and
  - [ ] confirm no SoulSearch window opened during startup itself.
- [ ] Restart again and confirm the binding remains singular and functional.
- [ ] Replace the default with a custom `gui/soulsearch` binding through
      `gui/keybinds`, save, restart, and confirm the custom binding remains and
      the default is not recreated.
- [ ] Remove all SoulSearch bindings through `gui/keybinds`, save, restart, and
      confirm bootstrap respects the persisted opt-out.
- [ ] Invoke manual `soulsearch` after opt-out and confirm it does not recreate
      the binding unless a separately documented explicit reset operation was
      intentionally added.
- [ ] Exercise `disable soulsearch` and confirm:
  - [ ] `isEnabled()`/the `enable` listing reports disabled;
  - [ ] existing screens and bindings remain untouched;
  - [ ] `gui/soulsearch` still opens normally; and
  - [ ] overlay visibility/configuration is unchanged.
- [ ] Cold-restart after `disable soulsearch` and confirm the documented
      session-only behavior: automatic bootstrap is active again by default,
      while existing, custom, or opted-out binding state remains unchanged.
- [ ] Exercise `enable soulsearch` repeatedly and confirm it is idempotent and
      does not defeat the binding opt-out.
- [ ] Run `soulsearch reload` while bootstrap is enabled and disabled in
      separate passes; confirm enabled state is preserved, no UI reopens, no
      binding duplicates appear, and the next `gui/soulsearch` uses rebuilt
      modules.
- [ ] With the Stats popover package work present, confirm the unit-card widget
      is discovered before any manual SoulSearch command, honors saved widget
      state, and opens the popover independently of the full-window keybind.
- [ ] When the Stats popover package work is present, disable the global overlay
      framework once and confirm SoulSearch does not re-enable it; restore the
      user's prior setting afterward.
- [ ] Confirm startup produces no routine SoulSearch console noise and no error
      in `stderr.log`.
- [ ] Restore all isolated user configuration after acceptance.

### Phase 6 gate

- [ ] A new user can install the packaged mod and use both normal SoulSearch
      entry paths without knowing or running an initialization command.
- [ ] Existing user binding and overlay choices remain authoritative across
      restart, enable/disable, explicit setup, and reload.
- [ ] Live evidence is dated and records the package version/path and target
      DFHack version.

## Completion criteria

- [ ] Automatic bootstrap is minimal, non-visual, idempotent, and fail-soft for
      optional startup setup.
- [ ] Full initialization stays lazy and defensive at explicit UI/data entry
      points.
- [ ] The default keybinding is seeded once without overriding customization or
      recreating a saved removal.
- [ ] Native enable/disable state has a narrow documented meaning and does not
      conflict with explicit commands or overlay configuration.
- [ ] Clean packaged installation requires no manual SoulSearch command.
- [ ] Automated and live gates pass, and the parent item in
      `docs/project.todo` is marked complete with the validation date.

